ZERO BOOTABLE DISK PAYLOAD v1

This payload is constructed directly from original Zero artifacts. It is not
an ISO9660 filesystem or a renamed ISO. The distributable .zmedia container
and its integrity checks are specified separately. A flash writer validates
the container and writes this payload from byte zero of the selected device.

Size:18874368 bytes, exactly36864 logical sectors of512 bytes.
Devices exposing4096-byte logical sectors are unsupported.512-byte logical
sectors on4096-byte physical storage are compatible with this layout.

Sector ranges, inclusive:
0             MBR with original Zero bootstrap and one ESP partition entry
1..31         original Zero BIOS second stage, loaded to0000:7e00
32..959       x64 kernel slot475136 bytes
960..1951     IA32 kernel slot507904 bytes
1952..3999    original compiler/tool bundle1048576 bytes
4000..4095    zero padding
4096..36863   FAT16 EFI system partition16777216 bytes

The single MBR entry has active flag80, typeEF, startLBA4096 and32768 sectors.
CHS values are saturated; LBA is authoritative. Disk signature5a4d0001 is
deterministic and is not a per-installation identity. Firmware support uses
the standard PC interfaces implemented by our code: BIOS EDD reads and UEFI
removable-media paths EFI/BOOT/BOOTX64.EFI and EFI/BOOT/BOOTIA32.EFI.
The ESP hidden-sector field is4096. FAT content is built by media/disk-fat.zero
from the same original OS loaders, kernels and bundle used by the project,
plus the new original Zero media menu and firmware flash application.

UEFI presents a Zero menu:1 Start Zero OS,2 Flash Writer. Enter or a3-second
timeout starts Zero. The menu builds a bounded full firmware device path,
then uses UEFI LoadImage/StartImage to invoke the selected original program.
StartImage runs on a separately allocated1MiB continuation stack. Each Zero
firmware application reserves its own64KiB arena; this separation prevents
nested applications from exhausting a small IA32 firmware-provided stack.
Returned applications release their private stack and return to the menu.
BIOS directly starts the existing kernel; the firmware flash application
requires UEFI. The menu is source-authored in Zero for both pointer widths.
Original OS loaders are stored as EFI/BOOT/OSX64.EFI and OSIA32.EFI. Default
BOOTX64/BOOTIA32 paths contain the menus. Writer names are FLASHX64.EFI and
FLASH32.EFI; the latter is the FAT8.3 alias for build artifact FLASHIA32.EFI.
Menu slots are64KiB each; writer slots128KiB each. Added FAT clusters4228..4995
follow the original tool bundle without changing the kernel or BIOS ranges.

The writer loads root system.zmedia from a separate firmware-readable source
filesystem. The boot ESP does not contain its own recursively nested image.
Both the boot-utility device and actual source-image device are protected by
the writer. Its source selection and raw-write rules are documented separately.

BIOS startup is source-authored in Zero. disk-bootstrap.zero is compiled with
--real. disk.zero checks the compiler header and final halt, retains the
compiler-produced far-jump encoding, links its string-free body into the MBR,
normalizes CS, and replaces its final halt with the second-stage entry jump.
The linked code must end before the disk signature at byte440. The permitted
compiled bootstrap size is580..946 bytes including its512-byte header; the
current artifact is743 bytes. No assembler source or external linker is used.
The bootstrap reads31 sectors fromLBA1 to7e00. It halts on read failure.

disk-bios.zero retains the existing E820/EBDA checks, CPU architecture choice,
VBE graphics negotiation, bundle loading, and protected/long-mode handoff.
Its disk reads use64 sectors per32KiB block and32 for final16KiB kernel tails.
This preserves the original low-memory safety bounds for both architectures.

BUILD INTERFACE

zero-disk RELEASE_DIR BOOTSTRAP_BINARY DISK_BIOS_BINARY NEW_DISK_IMAGE

Inputs must be built from trusted original project sources. RELEASE_DIR holds
BOOTX64.EFI, BOOTIA32.EFI, kernel.bin, kernel32.bin, tools.bin, MENU64.EFI,
MENU32.EFI, FLASHX64.EFI, FLASHIA32.EFI and media-fat64.app.
The native tool executes the validated original media-fat64.app in a bounded own
memory-stream context. This is a trusted-code build step, not a sandbox for
arbitrary executable inputs. Linux and Windows builds use identical source.

Every input is size-bounded, read exactly, probed for excess bytes, and checked
for size changes. All six EFI artifacts have architecture/header checks and
raw-section bounds validated against their actual file length before padding;
a header-only executable is refused. The destination is exclusively created; existing files are
preserved. After writing, the tool flushes, closes, reopens and compares every
byte, checks EOF and final size. Failed newly created outputs are retained
with an explicit diagnostic. The builder never unlinks a path after closing
its output handle, because another file could have replaced that path.
This builder only creates regular files. It does not open physical devices;
device selection, write confirmation and raw-device safety belong to the
separate flash writer.

No claim of a runtime USB driver in the current Zero kernel follows from
firmware USB boot support. BIOS/UEFI read USB devices before transferring
control to Zero; native kernel USB storage is a separate capability.
